 "drivingQuestion": "From where do we know the two thanks-offerings are their loaves?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "derivation from 'large' is analysed in shevuot.sugya045",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "shevuot.15a.6",
   "daf": "15a",
   "sentenceId": 5,
   "he": "וּבִשְׁתֵּי תּוֹדוֹת. תָּנָא: שְׁתֵּי תּוֹדוֹת שֶׁאָמְרוּ – בְּלַחְמָן וְלֹא בִּבְשָׂרָן. מְנָהָנֵי מִילֵּי? אָמַר רַב חִסְדָּא, דְּאָמַר קְרָא: ״וָאַעֲמִידָה שְׁתֵּי תוֹדֹת גְּדוֹלֹת וְתַהֲלֻכֹת לַיָּמִין מֵעַל לַחוֹמָה״.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «And with two thanks-offerings.» A tanna «taught» in a baraita: «And» with regard to the «two thanks-offerings that are mentioned» here, the reference is «to their loaves, but not to their flesh.» An animal thanks-offering is accompanied by forty loaves of bread that are brought as a meal-offering. Ten loaves are leavened, and the remainder is comprised of ten each of three types of unleavened bread. The Gemara asks: «From where are these matters» derived? «Rav Ḥisda said: As the verse states» with regard to the consecration of the wall of Jerusalem in the days of Ezra: «“And I placed two large thanks-offerings, and we went in procession to the right upon the wall”» (Nehemiah 12:31)."
  }
 ],
